# Observer

When one object changes, many dependents update **without** the subject listing them.

## Anatomy

- Publisher emits a typed event.
- Event is a data payload.
- Observers subscribe; publisher has zero imports of observers.

Nest’s `@nestjs/event-emitter` is this pattern:

```ts
@Injectable()
export class OrderService {
  constructor(private readonly eventEmitter: EventEmitter2) {}

  createOrder(data: CreateOrderData) {
    const event = new OrderCreatedEvent({ ...data, orderId });
    this.eventEmitter.emit('order.created', event);
    return { orderId };
  }
}

@Injectable()
export class InventoryObserver {
  @OnEvent('order.created')
  handleOrderCreated(event: OrderCreatedEvent) {
    // decrement stock — OrderService does not know this class exists
  }
}
```

Register `EventEmitterModule.forRoot()` and every observer as a provider.

## Nest mapping

This repo: `SpaceEventsService` + Redis pubsub notifies live clients when files change. Workflows publish; they do not import GraphQL subscribers.

```ts
// after claim — CompleteSpaceFileUploadWorkflow
await this.spaceEvents.broadcast(/* file ready */);
```

- In-process side effects → `EventEmitter2` / domain events.
- Cross-instance / websocket → pubsub (`packages/nest/src/pubsub`).
- Ordered, must-succeed steps (promote then claim) stay in the **workflow**, not observers. Observers are for decoupled reactions (analytics, email, live UI).

## When to use

- Many reactions; set of listeners changes over time.
- Publisher must not grow a constructor full of collaborators.

## When not to use

- One subscriber — call it.
- Strict order + return values — Mediator or Facade.
- Observers that must run in the same transaction as the write — call a use-case in the workflow instead.

Related: `nest-mediator.mdc`, `nest-command.mdc`.
